(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"A statement is a sentence that is either true or false, but not both. Its truth value is T or F. Questions, commands and exclamations are not statements, and neither are open sentences whose truth depends on an unknown, such as x + 3 = 5.","w":["A sentence like 'x + 2 = 7' is not a statement until x is given a value."]},"1.2":{"s":"Simple statements are joined into compound statements by the connectives and (conjunction), or (disjunction), if-then (implication) and if and only if (biconditional). A conjunction is true only when both parts are true; a disjunction is false only when both parts are false. An implication is false only when the first part is true and the second is false.","w":["'If p then q' is TRUE whenever p is false; it fails only for p true and q false."]},"1.3":{"s":"A truth table lists every combination of truth values of the simple statements and the resulting value of a compound statement. With n simple statements there are 2ⁿ rows. The negation of a statement has the opposite truth value. The negation of 'p and q' is 'not p or not q', and of 'p or q' is 'not p and not q' (De Morgan's laws).","w":["Negating 'and' gives 'or' (and the other way round), with each part negated."]},"1.4":{"s":"A statement pattern is a compound statement built from letters p, q, r and connectives. Two patterns are logically equivalent if their truth tables are identical in the last column. Useful equivalences include p → q ≡ ~p ∨ q, the contrapositive ~q → ~p, and the biconditional as two implications. The converse and inverse are not equivalent to the original implication.","w":["p → q is equivalent to its contrapositive ~q → ~p, not to its converse q → p."]},"1.5":{"s":"A statement pattern is a tautology if it is true in every row of its truth table, a contradiction if it is false in every row, and a contingency if it is sometimes true and sometimes false. The negation of a tautology is a contradiction and the other way round.","w":["p ∨ ~p is a tautology; p ∧ ~p is a contradiction."]},"1.6":{"s":"A quantifier says how many objects a statement covers. The universal quantifier means 'for all' and the existential quantifier means 'there exists'. The negation of 'for all' is 'there exists one for which it fails', and the negation of 'there exists' is 'for all it fails'. The dual of a statement swaps and with or, and tautology with contradiction.","w":["Negating 'for every x' gives 'for some x … not', not 'for no x'."]},"1.7":{"s":"Switches in an electric circuit act like statements: a closed switch is true (1) and an open switch is false (0). Switches in series correspond to 'and' and switches in parallel to 'or'. A circuit's switching table lists whether current flows for each setting of the switches, so logic can be used to simplify circuits.","w":["Series = AND (both must be closed); parallel = OR (either may be closed)."]}};
